Also, to keep things machine-friendly for whoever's collecting the picks, please drop the final selection into a tiny JSON object like this — super simple:

{
  "solution": [<selected_item>, <selected_item>, ...]
}

Think of "solution" as just the list of picnic spots we pick — one identifier per spot. This JSON is just a sketch of the shape I expect (not the real answer yet), so when you fill it in use the actual IDs from the instance.

Please make sure you use the identifiers exactly as they appear in the input — no renaming, no made-up labels. 
- for example: "Valid identifiers look like plain numbers such as “1” or “23”, single capital letters like “A” or “B”, or a capital letter followed by digits like “A1” or “X7”. "

One afternoon the counselor sketched out that the best group wouldn't be made of similar students but of ones who complement each other in contrasting ways. The instruction was to select exactly the fixed number of students, and to check a group's quality the counselor adds up how far apart every pair in that group is — the higher that summed amount, the more diverse the group feels. Every slot has to be filled by a different student and the group size can’t be changed. The concrete instance and detailed pairwise differences are listed below.

